          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In as far as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from input data to output result, a
            %em reversible computation
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible"} invertible
            function, where the input may be recovered from knowledge of the output. More generally, as far as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            is any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
            (under the
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+trilogy"} computational trilogy
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          %p
            Typical operations of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+computer"} classical computer
            are not reversible: For example standard logical gates such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AND"} AND
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/XOR"} XOR
            etc. are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^2 \longrightarrow \{0,1\}
            and as such not
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible"} invertible
            = succeed "-gate" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NOT"} NOT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\} \xrightarrow{\sim} \{0,1\}
            (which switches its single input bit) and the
            = succeed "-gate" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CNOT"} CNOT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mn 2
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^2 \xrightarrow{\sim} \{0,1\}^2
            (which keeps the first “control” bit and switches the second bit if the first equals 1).
          %div{:style => "float:left;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/LeibnizMachineReversible.jpg", :width => "550"}/
          %p#ExampleOfIncrementalAddition
            Using just these reversible NOT- and CNOT-gates one may for instance implement the operation of
            %em
              incremental
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/addition"} addition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)+n \;\colon\; \mathbb{Z} \xrightarrow{\sim} \mathbb{Z}
            by a fixed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kaye04"} Kaye 2004
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mn 2
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-) + (-) \,\colon\, \mathbb{Z}^2 \xrightarrow{\;} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inverse
            being the corresponding incremental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subtraction"} subtraction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)-n \;\colon\; \mathbb{Z} \xrightarrow{\sim} \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            This state of affairs was reflected, for instance, in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gottfried+Leibniz"} Gottfried Leibniz
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stepped_reckoner"} stepped reckoner
            machine, which performs incremental addition when its crank is turned in one direction, and the inverse incremental subtraction when the crank is turned back in the opposite direction.
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            But the NOT- and CNOT-gates by themselves are not universal. On the other hand, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CCNOT"} CCNOT
            gate
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mn 3
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^3 \xrightarrow{\sim} \{0,1\}^3
            (which regards the first two inputs as control bits and switches the third input if these two are both 1) is both invertible as well as universal (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#NielsenChuang00"} Nielsen &amp; Chuang 2000, §1.4.1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}^{n_1} \xrightarrow{\;} \{0,1\}^{n_2}
            is equal to a circuit of CCNOT gates pre-composed with some cartesian power of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0\} \xhookrightarrow{\;} \{0,1\}
            (introducing auxiliary “bath” or “ancilla” bits) and post-composed with some cartesian power of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo →
                  %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\} \to \ast
            (deleting auxiliary bits). In other words: Every irreversible classical computation on some computing system may be understood as a reversible computation on the system coupled to an ambient “bath” together with a loss of information of computation output into this bath.
          %p
            Indeed, in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physical
            implementation of computation, the distinction between reversible and irreversible computation is essentially that of reversible or irreversible dynamical processes as known from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            = succeed "-increase" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entropy"} entropy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            is known as
            %em Landauer’s principle
            (due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Landauer61"} Landauer 1961
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#NielsenChuang00"} Nielsen &amp; Chuang 2000, §3.2.5
          %p
            While the notion of reversible computation was originally conceived in the context of classical computation, more recently its key example is understood to be
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"} quantum computation
            = succeed "cluding" do
              %em ex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+function+collapse"} collapses
            the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functions"} linear functions
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary operator
            and hence necessarily
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible"} invertible
            (see
            %a{:href => "#Frank03"} Frank 2003
            for emphasis).
          %p
            Therefore, the practical problem of implementing quantum computation faces analogous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamical
            issues as embodied by Landauer’s principle, which are the reason for the need of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+error+correction"} quantum error correction
            and/or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum stabilization
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+computation"}> quantum computation
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuit"} quantum circuit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nondeterministic+computation"} nondeterministic computation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+computing"} parallel computing
